---
title: Full-text search with ParadeDB
description: Rank search results by relevance in Prisma Postgres with ParadeDB's pg_search extension and a BM25 index, from SQL or Prisma ORM.
url: /postgres/database/paradedb
metaTitle: ParadeDB full-text search on Prisma Postgres
metaDescription: 'Enable ParadeDB pg_search on Prisma Postgres, create a BM25 index, and run ranked, phrase, and fuzzy searches from SQL, Prisma ORM 8, or Prisma ORM 7.'
---

Prisma Postgres includes `pg_search`, the PostgreSQL extension from [ParadeDB](https://www.paradedb.com). It adds a BM25 index, which ranks matching rows by how well they match a search query, the way a search engine orders its results. On this page you enable the extension, index the text columns you want to search, and run ranked searches from SQL or from Prisma ORM.

## Check the `pg_search` version first

{/* cspell:ignore migratoin */}

ParadeDB's current documentation and its `paradedb/paradedb:latest` Docker image describe `pg_search` 0.20 and later, but Prisma Postgres runs 0.17. An example copied from ParadeDB's documentation may fail on Prisma Postgres, so use the `@@@` operator and the `paradedb.*` functions shown on this page.

The newer releases added search operators (`|||`, `&&&`, `###`, `===`) and functions in a `pdb` schema, such as `pdb.score`, which are not available on Prisma Postgres yet. A query that uses them fails with `operator does not exist: text ||| unknown` or `schema "pdb" does not exist`.

To see which version your database offers, run:

```sql
SELECT name, default_version, installed_version
FROM pg_available_extensions
WHERE name = 'pg_search';
```

```text no-copy
   name    | default_version | installed_version
-----------+-----------------+-------------------
 pg_search | 0.17.12         |
```

`installed_version` stays empty until you run `CREATE EXTENSION` in the next section.

## Prerequisites

- A Prisma Postgres database and its connection string. To get one without an account, run `npx create-db@latest`; see [`npx create-db`](/postgres/npx-create-db).
- A way to run SQL against it, such as `psql`, a GUI client like TablePlus, or a migration file.

## Search with SQL

The steps in this section work from any PostgreSQL client. They use an `Article` table with a `title` and a `body`.

### 1. Enable the extension

Install `pg_search` in your database, which also creates the `paradedb` schema that holds the search functions.

```sql
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_search;
```

### 2. Create a table and a BM25 index

A BM25 index covers every column you want to search, plus a key column that identifies each row. Name the key column in `key_field` and put it first inside `USING bm25 (...)`. Usually it is the primary key.

```sql
CREATE TABLE "Article" (
  id    serial PRIMARY KEY,
  title text NOT NULL,
  body  text NOT NULL
);

INSERT INTO "Article" (title, body) VALUES
  ('Prisma ORM 8 is here', 'A new query builder and a migration graph'),
  ('Migrations explained', 'How the migration graph tracks every migration you apply'),
  ('Running shoes', 'Lightweight running shoes for the road');

CREATE INDEX article_search ON "Article"
USING bm25 (id, title, body)
WITH (key_field = 'id');
```

PostgreSQL keeps the index up to date as you insert, update, and delete rows, so a new row is searchable as soon as its transaction commits.

### 3. Search a column

`column @@@ 'query'` matches the rows whose column contains the words in the query. Matching ignores case.

```sql
SELECT id, title FROM "Article" WHERE body @@@ 'migration graph';
```

```text no-copy
 id |        title
----+----------------------
  1 | Prisma ORM 8 is here
  2 | Migrations explained
```

By default a row matches when it contains any word of the query. The query string accepts the following syntax:

| Query | Matches rows whose column contains |
| --- | --- |
| `'migration graph'` | `migration` or `graph` |
| `'+running +road'` | both `running` and `road` |
| `'"migration graph"'` | the words `migration graph` next to each other, in that order |
| `'migration AND road'` | both words, written with `AND` instead of `+` |

Search matches whole words only, so `'migrations'` does not match a row that contains only `migration`.

To search several columns, combine conditions with `OR`:

```sql
SELECT id, title FROM "Article" WHERE title @@@ 'migrations' OR body @@@ 'shoes';
```

### 4. Rank results by relevance

`paradedb.score(key)` returns each matching row's BM25 score, higher for a better match. Pass it the key column and sort by it:

```sql
SELECT id, title, paradedb.score(id) AS score
FROM "Article"
WHERE body @@@ 'migration graph'
ORDER BY score DESC
LIMIT 10;
```

```text no-copy
 id |        title         |   score
----+----------------------+------------
  2 | Migrations explained |  1.0549045
  1 | Prisma ORM 8 is here | 0.92357993
```

The second article ranks first because its body mentions `migration` twice.

### 5. Use the query functions for fuzzy and exact searches

For searches the query string does not cover, write `key @@@ paradedb.<function>(...)`. With these functions, the left side of `@@@` is the key column, and the column you search is the function's first argument, written as a string.

```sql
-- Allow up to two typos per word
SELECT id FROM "Article"
WHERE id @@@ paradedb.match('body', 'migratoin', distance => 2);

-- Words next to each other, in order
SELECT id FROM "Article"
WHERE id @@@ paradedb.phrase('body', ARRAY['migration', 'graph']);

-- One word exactly as it is stored in the index, so write it in lowercase
SELECT id FROM "Article"
WHERE id @@@ paradedb.term('body', 'migration');
```

To show the reader where a row matched, `paradedb.snippet(column)` returns the matching text with each hit wrapped in `<b>` tags:

```sql
SELECT id, paradedb.snippet(body) FROM "Article" WHERE body @@@ 'shoes';
```

```text no-copy
 id |                     snippet
----+-------------------------------------------------
  3 | Lightweight running <b>shoes</b> for the road
```

## Use ParadeDB with Prisma ORM 8

With the experimental `@prisma/orm-extension-paradedb` package, Prisma ORM 8 declares the BM25 index in your contract, the TypeScript file that describes your tables, and installs `pg_search` through a migration. First set up the project as the [ParadeDB extension page](/orm/extensions/paradedb) describes: it covers installing the package and declaring the index. Then make the two Prisma Postgres changes below.

### Add the `paradedb` schema to the search path

Without this change, `npx prisma db init` installs the extension and creates the index, but `npx prisma db verify`, which checks that the database matches your contract, fails afterwards, and so does `npx prisma db sign`. The reason is that `pg_search` 0.17 always installs into a schema called `paradedb`, while `db verify` expects the extension in a schema on the connection's search path, the list of schemas PostgreSQL looks in. On Prisma Postgres that list holds only `public` and the system schema `pg_catalog`:

```text no-copy
✘ mismatch: database/pg_search. extension "pg_search" is installed in schema "paradedb",
  which is not on the search path (pg_catalog, public).
```

Add the schema to the database's search path once, from any SQL client. A Prisma Postgres database is named `postgres`, as the end of its connection string shows:

```sql
ALTER DATABASE postgres SET search_path = "$user", public, paradedb;
```

The setting applies only to connections opened after the change. To confirm it, run `db verify` again:

```bash
npx prisma db verify
```

```text no-copy
✔ Database marker and schema match contract
```

### Search with the operations Prisma Postgres supports

On `pg_search` 0.17, `paradeDbMatch` works as the [ParadeDB extension page](/orm/extensions/paradedb#search) describes. The other operations fail, and this table lists what to use instead:

| Operation | On Prisma Postgres | Instead |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `paradeDbMatch(query)` | Works | |
| `paradeDbMatchAny(query)` | Fails | `paradeDbMatch(query)`, which also matches any word |
| `paradeDbMatchAll(query)` | Fails | `paradeDbMatch('+word +word')` |
| `paradeDbPhrase(query)` | Fails | `paradeDbMatch('"word word"')` |
| `paradeDbTerm(word)` | Fails | `fns.raw` with `paradedb.term`, shown below |
| `paradeDbScore(key)` | Fails | `fns.raw` with `paradedb.score`, shown below |
| `paradeDbFuzzy`, `paradeDbBoost`, and the proximity operations | Fail | `fns.raw` with `paradedb.match(..., distance => n)` for fuzzy matching |

`paradeDbMatch` accepts the same query string syntax as `@@@` in SQL, so phrase and must-contain searches work through it:

```ts
const articles = await db.orm.public.Article.where((a) =>
  a.body.paradeDbMatch('"migration graph"'),
).all();
```

To rank results, write the query with the [SQL query builder](/orm/reference/sql-query-builder) and compute the score with a [raw fragment](/orm/reference/raw-queries), `fns.raw`, that calls `paradedb.score`:

```ts
const plan = db.sql.public.Article.select('id', 'title')
  .select('score', (f, fns) => fns.raw`paradedb.score(${f.id})`.returns('pg/float4@1'))
  .where((f, fns) => fns.paradeDbMatch(f.body, 'migration graph'))
  .orderBy((f, fns) => fns.raw`paradedb.score(${f.id})`.returns('pg/float4@1'), { direction: 'desc' })
  .limit(10)
  .build();

const ranked = await db.query(plan).toArray();
```

```js no-copy
[
  { id: 2, title: 'Migrations explained', score: 1.0549045 },
  { id: 1, title: 'Prisma ORM 8 is here', score: 0.92357993 },
]
```

`.returns('pg/float4@1')` tells Prisma ORM that the fragment returns a floating-point number. The score expression appears in both `select` and `orderBy` because each call builds its own fragment.

Fuzzy and exact-word searches use the `paradedb.match` and `paradedb.term` functions from step 5 the same way, inside `where`. Interpolate the search text with `${...}` so Prisma ORM sends it as a query parameter:

```ts
const fuzzy = db.sql.public.Article.select('id', 'title')
  .where((f, fns) => fns.raw`${f.id} @@@ paradedb.match('body', ${'migratoin'}, distance => 2)`.returns('pg/bool@1'))
  .build();

const exact = db.sql.public.Article.select('id', 'title')
  .where((f, fns) => fns.raw`${f.id} @@@ paradedb.term('body', ${'migration'})`.returns('pg/bool@1'))
  .build();

const rows = await db.query(fuzzy).toArray();
```

## Use ParadeDB with Prisma ORM 7

Prisma ORM 7 has no BM25 index type, so you create the index in SQL and search with [raw queries](/orm/v7/prisma-client/using-raw-sql). Generate the migration for your `Article` model with `npx prisma migrate dev --create-only`, add the `CREATE EXTENSION` and `CREATE INDEX` statements from steps 1 and 2 to the end of its `migration.sql`, and apply it with `npx prisma migrate dev`. [Customizing migrations](/orm/v7/prisma-migrate/workflows/customizing-migrations) walks through the same flow.

Search with `$queryRaw`:

```ts
const query = 'migration graph';
const articles = await prisma.$queryRaw`
  SELECT id, title, paradedb.score(id) AS score
  FROM "Article"
  WHERE body @@@ ${query}
  ORDER BY score DESC
  LIMIT 10
`;
```

Prisma Migrate does not know about BM25 indexes, so when it compares your schema with the database, it plans `DROP INDEX "article_search";` next to your real changes. Create each later migration with `--create-only` as well, and delete that statement from its `migration.sql` before you apply it.

## Develop locally

The local Prisma Postgres database that `npx prisma dev` starts does not include `pg_search`. To search on your machine, run ParadeDB's Docker image at the version Prisma Postgres runs, so a query that works locally also works in production:

```bash
docker run --name paradedb -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres -p 127.0.0.1:5432:5432 -d paradedb/paradedb:0.17.12-pg17
```

Connect to it with `postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/postgres`, then run the same setup as on Prisma Postgres: the `CREATE EXTENSION` statement or your migrations, plus the search path change if you use Prisma ORM 8. Avoid the `latest` tag, because it runs a newer `pg_search` that accepts syntax Prisma Postgres rejects; see [Check the `pg_search` version first](#check-the-pg_search-version-first).

